---
import Band from "../components/Band.astro";
import Card from "../components/Card.astro";
import DownloadButtons from "../components/DownloadButtons.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import SectionHead from "../components/SectionHead.astro";
import Shot from "../components/Shot.astro";
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { WEB_APP } from "../site";

import create from "../assets/create.webp";
import dark from "../assets/dark.webp";
import devices from "../assets/devices.webp";
import hero from "../assets/hero.webp";
import launch from "../assets/launch.webp";
import phone from "../assets/phone.webp";
import plain from "../assets/plain.webp";
import row from "../assets/row.webp";
import stack from "../assets/stack.webp";
import transplant from "../assets/transplant.webp";

// Widths the layout gives a full-width block on a phone.
const PHONE = "(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 2.5rem)";

// The numbered pins over the sidebar row, placed on the image in
// percent, in the order the legend lists them.
const PINS = [
  { x: "28%", y: "11%", part: "The project" },
  { x: "57.5%", y: "13%", part: "The Mac it lives on, when that's not this one" },
  { x: "86%", y: "11%", part: "Last activity" },
  { x: "2.6%", y: "51%", part: "The branch" },
  { x: "56.5%", y: "72%", part: "Files with uncommitted changes" },
  { x: "66.5%", y: "72%", part: "Commits not pushed yet" },
  { x: "84%", y: "72%", part: "Its pull request, and its place in a stack" },
  { x: "37.5%", y: "76%", part: "The folder" },
];
---

<Page>
  <Band color="mint" wall="leaf" kanji="mori" kanjiClass="hero-kanji" class="hero">
    <div class="wrap hero-copy">
      <h1>A Mac app for managing git worktrees</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Create a worktree in two clicks, open it in the tools you already use,
        and see where each branch stands, whether it's on this Mac or another
        one of yours.
      </p>
      <DownloadButtons />
      <p class="fine">
        For Apple Silicon Macs. Signed, notarized, free and open source.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap wide stage">
      <Shot
        src={hero}
        frame="window"
        class="stage-main"
        priority
        sizes={`${PHONE}, min(1075px, 84vw - 2.1rem)`}
        alt="The app's window: a mint sidebar listing worktrees across projects, each with its changed files, commits ahead and pull request number. The open worktree shows launcher buttons, its pull request with checks passed and a Merge button, and its recent commits."
      />
      <Shot
        src={row}
        frame="framed"
        class="stage-row"
        sizes="min(310px, 24vw)"
        alt=""
      />
      <Shot
        src={phone}
        frame="phone"
        class="stage-phone"
        eager
        sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(34vw - 1.85rem), min(240px, 20vw - 1.5rem)"
        alt="The same worktrees in the web app on a phone."
      />
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band>
    <div class="wrap narrow center">
      <h2>Worktrees are great. The chores around them aren't.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        <code>git worktree</code> gives every branch its own folder, so an agent
        can work in one while you review a pull request in another. Setting each
        one up by hand is the slow part, and so is cleaning up after.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap compare">
      <div>
        <p class="compare-label">By hand</p>
        <pre
          class="terminal"><code><span class="p">$</span> git worktree add -b fix-login \
    ../app-fix-login main
<span class="p">$</span> cp .env.local ../app-fix-login/
<span class="p">$</span> cd ../app-fix-login
<span class="p">$</span> pnpm install
<span class="p">$</span> cursor .

<span class="c"># a week later, for each one</span>
<span class="p">$</span> git worktree remove ../app-fix-login
<span class="p">$</span> git branch -D fix-login</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
        <Icon name="arrow-right" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="compare-label">In Shigoto no Mori</p>
        <Shot
          src={create}
          frame="card-shot"
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) calc(100vw - 2.5rem), 540px"
          alt="The New worktree button with a menu under it listing projects to create the worktree in."
        />
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>Click <b>New worktree</b></li>
          <li>Pick the project</li>
          <li>Carry-over and setup run for you</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band id="create" color="sand" wall="square" kanji="sakusei">
    <div class="wrap">
      <SectionHead kicker="Create" title="A new worktree in two clicks">
        You get a fresh branch off your default branch, in its own folder, ready
        to open. Everything a new checkout usually needs comes along.
      </SectionHead>
      <div class="cards four">
        <Card icon="copy" chip="mint" title="Carry-over">
          <p>
            List the gitignored files a worktree needs, like
            <code>.env.local</code>, and each new one gets a copy or a symlink.
            A <code>.worktreeinclude</code> file works too.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card icon="play" chip="amber" title="Setup and teardown">
          <p>
            Run <code>pnpm install</code>, or anything else, when a worktree is
            created, and your own cleanup when it's removed.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card icon="git-pull-request" chip="sky" title="Start from anywhere">
          <p>
            Hold Shift to branch from something other than the default branch,
            check out an existing branch, or start from an open pull request.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card icon="wand" chip="pink" title="Named for you">
          <p>Every worktree gets a placeholder name until you rename it:</p>
          <ul class="names" aria-label="Example names">
            <li>brave-badger</li>
            <li>gentle-gecko</li>
            <li>quiet-quail</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band wall="leaf" kanji="joutai">
    <div class="wrap">
      <SectionHead kicker="Keep track" title="See where every branch stands">
        Each worktree is one row in the sidebar. It shows enough to act on
        without opening anything.
      </SectionHead>
      <div class="anatomy">
        <Shot
          src={row}
          frame="framed"
          class="anatomy-row"
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) calc(100vw - 2.5rem), 620px"
          alt="One sidebar row: project shigoto-no-mori, a laptop badge, 40m ago, branch exp/terrier-sync, 7 changed files, 1 commit ahead, pull request #151 as 2 of 3, folder gentle-gecko."
        >
          {
            PINS.map((pin, i) => (
              <span class="pin" style={`--x: ${pin.x}; --y: ${pin.y}`}>
                {i + 1}
              </span>
            ))
          }
        </Shot>
        <ol class="legend">
          {PINS.map((pin) => <li>{pin.part}</li>)}
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="split">
        <Shot
          src={stack}
          frame="window"
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) calc(100vw - 2.5rem), 650px"
          alt="A worktree whose pull request is the first of a stack of three, marked Merged, with the other two listed above it and 'lands on main' below."
        />
        <div class="points">
          <div class="point">
            <Icon name="file-diff" class="chip mint" />
            <div>
              <h3>Review and commit</h3>
              <p>
                Read the diff of your uncommitted changes, one commit or the
                whole PR, then commit all of it or only part.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="point">
            <Icon name="git-merge" class="chip mint" />
            <div>
              <h3>Merge from the app</h3>
              <p>
                Merge, squash or rebase, whichever your repo allows, including a
                whole stack at once. Then remove the worktree in one more click.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="point">
            <Icon name="inbox" class="chip mint" />
            <div>
              <h3>An inbox for active work</h3>
              <p>
                Shelve what you're not touching this week, and merged worktrees
                fold away on their own.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band color="amber" wall="triangle" kanji="kidou">
    <div class="wrap">
      <SectionHead kicker="Launch" title="Opens in the tools you already use" center>
        Every worktree gets a row of buttons for the apps on your Mac: editors,
        terminals, Git clients and agent apps. Add your own shell commands too,
        per project or for all of them, like starting an agent CLI in that
        folder.
      </SectionHead>
      <Shot
        src={launch}
        frame="card-shot"
        class="launch"
        sizes={`${PHONE}, 720px`}
        alt="A worktree's launch row: buttons with app icons for cmux, Cursor, Finder, Ghostty, GitHub, GitHub Desktop, Rider, Terminal, VS Code, Xcode and Zed, and under them the project's package.json scripts."
      />
      <div class="launch-notes">
        <div class="keys" role="img" aria-label="Command 1 to Command 9">
          <kbd>⌘1</kbd><kbd>⌘2</kbd><kbd>⌘3</kbd><span>…</span><kbd>⌘9</kbd>
        </div>
        <p>
          The first nine have shortcuts. Scripts from <code>package.json</code>
          run in a built-in console with one click.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="note center">
        It doesn't replace your editor, terminal or agent. It opens them in the
        right folder.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band id="machines" color="sky" wall="apple" kanji="kiki">
    <div class="wrap">
      <SectionHead
        kicker="Other machines"
        title="Worktrees on your other Macs, without the networking"
        center
      >
        Sign in on each Mac and their worktrees show up in the same list, looking
        just like the local ones. Machines connect directly on the same network,
        or through a tunnel when they aren't. There's no VPN, SSH key or router
        setting to deal with.
      </SectionHead>
      <div class="cards three">
        <Card icon="cable" chip="sky" title="Forward a port">
          <p>
            Start a dev server on the other machine and open it on
            <code>localhost</code> here. The files stay where they are.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card icon="refresh-cw" chip="mint" title="Mirror a worktree">
          <p>
            Keep a worktree in sync on two machines: every file, not only what
            git tracks, in both directions. Edit on either side and git agrees
            on both.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card icon="shovel" chip="amber" title="Transplant it">
          <p>
            Move a worktree once, uncommitted changes included, then keep,
            shelve or remove the copy left behind.
          </p>
        </Card>
      </div>
      <div class="machines-stage">
        <Shot
          src={devices}
          frame="window"
          class="machines-main"
          sizes={`${PHONE}, min(905px, 78vw - 1.95rem)`}
          alt="The Devices page listing Studio Mac, Thinkpad and Mini with their connection state, their projects, and a port forwarded from Thinkpad."
        />
        <Shot
          src={transplant}
          frame="floating"
          class="machines-dialog"
          sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(92vw - 2.3rem), min(535px, 46vw - 1.15rem)"
          alt="The Transplant worktree dialog, moving fix-stale-locks from Studio Mac to Thinkpad with its carry-over files. A note says nothing on Studio Mac is deleted until the last step."
        />
      </div>
      <div class="cards two">
        <Card icon="smartphone" chip="sky" title="From a browser, or your phone" row>
          <p>
            <a href={WEB_APP}>{new URL(WEB_APP).host}</a> shows the same forest
            in any browser, with a layout made for phones. It doesn't hold
            projects itself. It works through the Macs on your account.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card
          icon="shield-check"
          chip="mint"
          title="Read-only until you say otherwise"
          row
        >
          <p>
            Other devices can see a machine's worktrees, but can't change
            anything on it until you turn on
            <b>Allow control from other devices</b> on that machine.
          </p>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band id="agents" color="wood" wall="square" kanji="meirei">
    <div class="wrap split agents">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">For agents</p>
        <h2>A CLI your agents can drive</h2>
        <p>
          The app comes with an <code>sm</code> command, mostly so coding agents
          can do what you do in the app: start a task in a fresh worktree, check
          its status, merge its PR and clean up, or hand it to another machine.
          Worktrees an agent makes show up in the app like any other.
        </p>
        <p>
          Install the skills and your agent learns the workflow without you
          spelling it out each time. The same commands work by hand, too.
        </p>
      </div>
      <pre
        class="terminal"><code><span class="c"># teach your agent the workflow</span>
<span class="p">$</span> npx skills add \
    https://github.com/sylophi/shigoto-no-mori

<span class="c"># what an agent runs for a task</span>
<span class="p">$</span> sm wt create
<span class="p">$</span> sm wt status

<span class="c"># move it to another machine</span>
<span class="p">$</span> sm wt send --to "Studio Mac"

<span class="c"># merge the PR and remove the worktree</span>
<span class="p">$</span> sm wt land</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band color="pink" wall="flower" kanji="gaikan">
    <div class="wrap">
      <SectionHead kicker="Looks" title="Soft by default, plain if you prefer" center>
        The default look borrows from Animal Crossing, which is where the name
        comes from: <i lang="ja-Latn">Dōbutsu no Mori</i> is "animal forest", and
        <i lang="ja-Latn">Shigoto no Mori</i> is "work forest". If that's not for
        you, a neutral theme is one setting away. Both come in light and dark.
      </SectionHead>
      <div class="looks">
        <Shot
          src={plain}
          frame="window"
          class="looks-back"
          sizes="min(870px, 75vw - 1.875rem)"
          alt="The neutral theme in light mode."
        />
        <Shot
          src={dark}
          frame="window"
          class="looks-front"
          sizes="min(870px, 75vw - 1.875rem)"
          alt="The default theme in dark mode."
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band wall="flower" kanji="dougu">
    <div class="wrap">
      <SectionHead kicker="Also included" title="The smaller things" />
      <div class="bento">
        <Card icon="trees" title="Tidy the forest" class="tile mint wide">
          <p>
            One page lists every worktree with its size, age and merge state.
            Only merged worktrees with nothing uncommitted are ticked for
            removal. Anything else you pick yourself.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card icon="download" title="Auto-pull" class="tile sky">
          <p>
            Mark a checkout, like your main one, and it fast-forwards from its
            upstream whenever the app fetches.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card icon="folder-input" title="Adopt existing worktrees" class="tile amber">
          <p>Worktrees made with plain git can be brought into the app.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card icon="sticky-note" title="Notes" class="tile pink">
          <p>Jot down what a worktree is for, right on its page.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card icon="archive" title="Shelve" class="tile sand">
          <p>Put a worktree out of sight without deleting anything.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card icon="badge-check" title="Quiet updates" class="tile mint wide">
          <p>
            Builds are signed and notarized. New versions download in the
            background and install the next time you restart.
          </p>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band id="limits" color="sand">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <p class="kicker">Before you download</p>
      <h2>What it doesn't do</h2>
      <dl class="limits">
        <div class="card">
          <dt>Mac only, Apple Silicon only.</dt>
          <dd>
            There are no Windows, Linux or Intel builds. The web app runs in any
            browser, but it needs a Mac on your account to work through.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <dt>GitHub only, through <code>gh</code>.</dt>
          <dd>
            Pull request status, merging and landing use the GitHub CLI. Other
            hosts aren't supported, and the app doesn't open pull requests for
            you.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <dt>Not an editor, terminal or agent.</dt>
          <dd>
            The built-in console runs your project's scripts, not a general
            shell. Everything else opens in the apps you already have.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <dt>An account only for the multi-machine part.</dt>
          <dd>Everything on a single Mac works without signing in.</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <dt>Made by one developer.</dt>
          <dd>
            It's built for the author's own daily work, so it's shaped by one
            person's habits and has no team features. Releases come often.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band color="mint" wall="leaf" kanji="brand" kanjiClass="closing-kanji" class="closing">
    <div class="wrap narrow center">
      <img class="closing-icon framed" src="/img/icon.png" alt="" width="96" height="96" />
      <h2>Download Shigoto no Mori</h2>
      <p class="fine">For Apple Silicon Macs. Free and open source.</p>
      <DownloadButtons />
    </div>
  </Band>
</Page>
